  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 353 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThis weekend...Upper troughing will lift north and east towards the \nCanadian Prairies as a cutoff low forms over the Northeast Coast. \nHeights aloft will build as a result and lead to a period of dry and \nwarmer weather. Daytime highs over the weekend will climb to the \nmiddle 70s to middle 80s across the local area with the warmest \nvalues in the west. A trailing front/surface trough will extend down \nthrough the Northern Plains and move into western Nebraska. This \nwill propel dry air off the high terrain into western Nebraska. This \nwill likely introduce some humidity minimums falling into the 20 to \n25 percent range for the Panhandle and perhaps as far east as our \nwestern zones. At this time, precise eastward extent of this drier \nair is somewhat uncertain but in any event, marginal winds should \npreclude greater fire weather concerns.\n\nNext week...the next disturbance to affect the region takes shape \nover the central valley of California by late Sunday/early Monday. \nAs this feature digs south, broad height falls will expand across \nthe Great Basin and into the central/northern Rockies. Resultant \nsouthwesterly flow will feed rich tropical moisture from Hurricane \nPolo through the Desert Southwest and into much of the Plains. As \nthe trough approaches by late Monday into Tuesday, rain and \nthunderstorm potential increases markedly with the model blend \nproducing 65-85%, or \"Definite\" categories, across much of western \nand central Nebraska. NBM QPF probabilities remain quite bullish \nwith 50%+ potential for exceeding a half an inch of rain along and \neast of Highway 61. While finer details will need sorted out, \nconfidence in wetting rains for at least portions of the area \ncontinue to increase though precisely how much will likely not be \nresolved until this weekend at the earliest. Lingering moisture and \nadditional upper-level impulses should allow rain potential to \ncontinue through the end of the forecast period. This will likely \noccur with moderating temperatures.",
